    <mj-text
      padding="10px 12px"
      font-family="Austin News Text Web, TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif"
      color="#000000"
    >
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin: 0;">
        Food routes under strain
      </h2>
    </mj-text>
    <mj-image
          border-radius="10px"
          padding="10px 12px"
          width="600px"
          src="https://www.dubaisummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
          alt="REPLACE_ME"
          href="https://www.dubaisummary.com/"
          target="_blank"
        />

    <mj-text padding="10px 12px 16px 12px" font-family="Arial" color="#000000">
      <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What’s happening?</strong> The Iran war is putting Gulf food security under serious pressure by snarling shipping routes and threatening key ports. Wealthy Gulf states import around <strong>80% to 90%</strong> of their food, and much of it normally moves through the Strait of Hormuz. That means a prolonged conflict could quickly turn a logistics problem into a price and supply problem, despite years of planning following the 2008 food crisis.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>On the ground:</strong> Analysts say more than<a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/gulf-food-strategy-tested-iran-war-snarls-shipping-routes-2026-03-05/" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #102341; color: black;"> 70% of GCC food imports pass through Hormuz</a>, making the region highly exposed to disruption. Iranian attacks this week hit major trade lifelines, including Jebel Ali, the Gulf’s biggest container port, temporarily suspending operations. Smaller ports outside the strait, such as Khorfakkan and Fujairah, have far lower capacity and cannot fully replace Jebel Ali or Abu Dhabi’s Khalifa Port. Supermarket shelves in the UAE remain mostly stocked for now, but restocking is taking longer; panic buying has already flared, and some residents report sudden price jumps, especially for perishables like bananas.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What’s next?</strong> Governments may rely on strategic reserves, subsidies, and rerouted supply chains, but if disruption drags on, longer lead times and higher food prices will be hard to avoid.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong><em>Fact:</em></strong><em> The UAE’s Fujairah grain silos, opened in </em><strong><em>2016</em></strong><em>, hold roughly </em><strong><em>300,000 metric tons</em></strong><em> outside Hormuz.</em></p>
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="10px" />
